It seems that `fdatasync`
```c
int fdatasync(int fd)
{
     return syscall_cp(SYS_fdatasync, fd);
}
// system/lib/libc/musl/arch/emscripten/bits/syscall.h
#define SYS_fdatasync		__syscall_fdatasync


// system/lib/wasmfs/syscalls.cpp
int __syscall_fdatasync(int fd) {
  // TODO: Optimize this to avoid unnecessarily flushing unnecessary metadata.
  // Translate from WASI positive error codes to negative error codes.
  return -__wasi_fd_sync(fd);
}
``` 

is practically the same as `fsync`
```c
int fsync(int fd)
{
#if __EMSCRIPTEN__
	return __wasi_syscall_ret(__wasi_fd_sync(fd));
#else
	return syscall_cp(SYS_fsync, fd);
#endif
}
```

but here we don't have SYS_fsync definition in `system/lib/libc/musl/arch/emscripten/bits/syscall.h`, so i assume it never goes into the else, and there you go you have your double definition 



I followed how fsync is defined but maybe we can go the other way around by applying how fdatasync was to fsync, i don't know.

I have runned test with 
```
test/runner.py 'other.test_unistd_fdatasync' \
               'other.test_fsync*' \
               'core0.test_unistd_misc*' \
               'lto0.test_unistd_misc*' \
               'lto2.test_unistd_misc*'
```

and everything pass

