issue.body:
**Version:** Emscripten 6.0.12 (emsdk), Node.js 24.19.0 (Memory64 on by default). Linux x86-64.

In a wasm64 module (`-m64`, `-sMAXIMUM_MEMORY=16GB`), emmalloc still has 32-bit limits:

1. `malloc()` of 4 GiB or more returns `NULL`, although the memory has room.
2. Once freed regions merge into one of 4 GiB or more, emmalloc computes their size and their free list
   wrongly: the next `free()` writes out of bounds (`RuntimeError: memory access out of bounds`; with
   assertions: `Assertion failed: bucketIndex < NUM_FREE_BUCKETS` or `region_is_free(...)`).

Both affect `-sMALLOC=mimalloc` as well, as mimalloc takes its arenas and its huge blocks from emmalloc
(`system/lib/mimalloc/src/prim/emscripten/prim.c`): there, too, `malloc(4 GiB)` returns `NULL`.
`-sMALLOC=dlmalloc` has neither problem.

## Reproduction

`repro.c`: test 1 allocates 3 GiB, 4 GiB − 64 MiB, 4 GiB and 5 GiB one after the other (each freed
again); test 2 allocates 12 blocks of 512 MiB (6 GiB), frees them all (they merge into one free region), and
allocates 12 blocks of 512 MiB again.

```c
// emmalloc in a wasm64 (-m64) module: blocks of 4 GiB or more, and free regions of 4 GiB or more.
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <emscripten/heap.h>

#define GiB ((size_t)1 << 30)
#define MiB ((size_t)1 << 20)

static void report(const char* what, void* p) {
  printf("%-44s -> %-4s (heap %5zu MiB of %zu)\n", what, p ? "ok" : "NULL", emscripten_get_heap_size() / MiB,
         emscripten_get_heap_max() / MiB);
  fflush(stdout);
}

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
  const int test = argc > 1 ? atoi(argv[1]) : 1;
  if (test == 1) {
    // 1. A block of 4 GiB or more: NULL, although the memory has room (16 GiB).
    const size_t sizes[] = { 3 * GiB, 4 * GiB - 64 * MiB, 4 * GiB, 5 * GiB };
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
      const size_t size = sizes[i];
      void* volatile p = malloc(size);
      char what[64];
      snprintf(what, sizeof what, "malloc(%zu MiB)", size / MiB);
      report(what, p);
      free(p);
    }
  } else {
    // 2. Free regions that merge into one of 4 GiB or more, then allocations again.
    enum { N = 12 };
    void* blocks[N];
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
      blocks[i] = malloc(512 * MiB);
      if (blocks[i]) memset(blocks[i], 1, 4096);
    }
    report("12 blocks of 512 MiB (6 GiB)", blocks[N - 1]);
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) free(blocks[i]);
    report("freed: one free region of about 6 GiB", (void*)1);
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
      blocks[i] = malloc(512 * MiB);
      char what[64];
      snprintf(what, sizeof what, "again malloc(512 MiB) #%d", i + 1);
      report(what, blocks[i]);
    }
  }
  return 0;
}
```

```bash
emcc -O2 -m64 -fno-builtin -sMALLOC=emmalloc -sALLOW_MEMORY_GROWTH -sMAXIMUM_MEMORY=16GB repro.c -o repro.js
node repro.js 1
node repro.js 2
```

(`-fno-builtin`: otherwise clang may remove a `malloc`/`free` pair and fold the `NULL` check.)

### Actual

```
=== -sMALLOC=emmalloc, test 1
malloc(3072 MiB)                             -> ok   (heap  3072 MiB of 16384)
malloc(4032 MiB)                             -> ok   (heap  7104 MiB of 16384)
malloc(4096 MiB)                             -> NULL (heap  7104 MiB of 16384)
malloc(5120 MiB)                             -> NULL (heap  7104 MiB of 16384)
=== -sMALLOC=emmalloc, test 2
12 blocks of 512 MiB (6 GiB)                 -> ok   (heap  6240 MiB of 16384)
RuntimeError: memory access out of bounds
=== -sMALLOC=mimalloc, test 1
malloc(3072 MiB)                             -> ok   (heap  3149 MiB of 16384)
malloc(4032 MiB)                             -> ok   (heap  7249 MiB of 16384)
malloc(4096 MiB)                             -> NULL (heap  7249 MiB of 16384)
malloc(5120 MiB)                             -> NULL (heap  7249 MiB of 16384)
```

(Test 1 also shows that emmalloc does not reuse the freed 3 GiB for the next block of 4 GiB − 64 MiB, which is
expected: they are not adjacent to free space at the top. Test 2 passes with mimalloc, which keeps the blocks in
its own arenas here, and both tests pass with dlmalloc.)

### Expected

As with `-sMALLOC=dlmalloc`: blocks of 4 and 5 GiB where the memory has room, and the freed 6 GiB reused
(`again malloc(512 MiB) #1` to `#12` -> ok, heap 6240 MiB).

## Causes (system/lib/emmalloc.c)

1. `#define MAX_ALLOC_SIZE 0xFFFFFFC7u`: `allocate_memory()` rejects every size above it, i.e. 4 GiB − 57 bytes,
   also in wasm64.
2. `#define FREE_REGION_FLAG 0x1u` is a 32-bit `unsigned int`, so `~FREE_REGION_FLAG` is `0xFFFFFFFE`. Where it
   masks a 64-bit `size_t` — `DECODE_CEILING_SIZE()`, and the size of the left neighbour in `emmalloc_free()`
   (`prevRegionSizeField & ~FREE_REGION_FLAG`) — it clears the upper 32 bits: a free region of 4 GiB or more
   loses its size. With `-DEMMALLOC_MEMVALIDATE`, after 8 of the 12 frees of test 2:
   `Used region 0x0000000000012ef0, size: 4294967424 (--FREE--) is corrupt (size markers in the beginning and at
   the end of the region do not match!)`, then `Assertion failed: region_is_free((Region*)region)` in
   `unlink_from_free_list()`.
3. `compute_free_list_bucket(size_t allocSize)` takes `__builtin_clz(allocSize)`, which truncates the size to 32
   bits: for a size of 4 GiB or more, `clz` comes from its low bits, while `allocSize >> (29 - clz)` uses all of
   them, and the bucket index lands far beyond `NUM_FREE_BUCKETS` (64): an out-of-bounds write into
   `freeRegionBuckets` (with assertions: `Assertion failed: bucketIndex < NUM_FREE_BUCKETS`). The same
   `__builtin_clz` of a `size_t` is in `emmalloc_compute_free_dynamic_memory_fragmentation_map()`.

The comment at the region layout still says `<size:uint32_t>`; the code uses `size_t` there, and so does the
struct `Region` (`RootRegion::size` is `uint32_t`, but overlays the `size_t` of a `Region` and is not read as size,
as far as I can see).

## Possible fix (tested)

```diff
--- a/system/lib/emmalloc.c
+++ b/system/lib/emmalloc.c
@@ -97,11 +97,11 @@
-#define FREE_REGION_FLAG 0x1u
+#define FREE_REGION_FLAG ((size_t)1) // wasm64 fix: ~FREE_REGION_FLAG must not clear the upper 32 bits of a size_t

-#define MAX_ALLOC_SIZE 0xFFFFFFC7u
+#define MAX_ALLOC_SIZE (SIZE_MAX - 0x38u) // wasm64 fix (0xFFFFFFC7u on wasm32)
@@ -244,7 +244,7 @@
 static int compute_free_list_bucket(size_t allocSize) {
   if (allocSize < 128) return (allocSize >> 3) - 1;
-  int clz = __builtin_clz(allocSize);
+  int clz = (allocSize >> 31 >> 1) ? 0 : __builtin_clz((unsigned)allocSize); // wasm64 fix: sizes of 4 GiB and more go to the last bucket
@@ -1407,7 +1407,7 @@
-        ++freeMemorySizeMap[31-__builtin_clz(freeDynamicMemory)];
+        ++freeMemorySizeMap[(freeDynamicMemory >> 31 >> 1) ? 31 : 31-__builtin_clz((unsigned)freeDynamicMemory)];
```

(`clz = 0` puts every region of 2 GiB or more into the last bucket, 63, as for 2-4 GiB today; on wasm32 the
three changes are no-ops: `SIZE_MAX - 0x38` is `0xFFFFFFC7`, and `allocSize >> 31 >> 1` is 0.) With it, compiled
with `-sMALLOC=none` (emmalloc.c and libc/sbrk.c added to the link), both tests pass, with
`-O0 -sASSERTIONS -DEMMALLOC_MEMVALIDATE` as with `-O2 -DNDEBUG`:

```
malloc(3072 MiB)                             -> ok   (heap  3072 MiB of 16384)
malloc(4032 MiB)                             -> ok   (heap  7104 MiB of 16384)
malloc(4096 MiB)                             -> ok   (heap  7104 MiB of 16384)
malloc(5120 MiB)                             -> ok   (heap  7104 MiB of 16384)
...
freed: one free region of about 6 GiB        -> ok   (heap  6240 MiB of 16384)
again malloc(512 MiB) #1 ... #12             -> ok   (heap  6240 MiB of 16384)
```

Without the `compute_free_list_bucket` change (the other two applied), test 2 still fails:
`Assertion failed: bucketIndex < NUM_FREE_BUCKETS`.

A test with blocks and free regions above 4 GiB in wasm64 (like `other.test_alloc_3GB`, for 4-8 GiB and
`-sMALLOC=emmalloc`/`mimalloc`) would catch this.

## Context

Found while building OpenMS for wasm64 (a mass-spectrometry library, whose searches need more than 4 GiB):
with `-sMALLOC=mimalloc`, no single block of 4 GiB or more could be allocated. OpenMS now builds Emscripten's
mimalloc itself for wasm64, on dlmalloc instead of emmalloc. Possibly related, but separate from this issue:
in wasm64, mimalloc aligns its arenas (page metadata) to 64 MiB and takes every larger block from emmalloc
directly, which with its exponentially growing arenas made a search's memory grow to several times its peak
use (12 GB for 1.2 GiB in use); built with `MI_FREE_USE_PAGEMAP` it stayed below 2 GB.

