At emcc built js file:
The MainLoop object updateStatus() method:
``` js
updateStatus() {
        if (Module['setStatus']) {
          var message = Module['statusMessage'] || 'Please wait...';
          var remaining = MainLoop.remainingBlockers ?? 0;
          var expected = MainLoop.expectedBlockers ?? 0;
          if (remaining) {
            if (remaining < expected) {
              Module['setStatus'](`{message} ({expected - remaining}/{expected})`);
            } else {
              Module['setStatus'](message);
            }
          } else {
            Module['setStatus']('');
          }
        }
      }
```
the line:
``` js
Module['setStatus'](`{message} ({expected - remaining}/{expected})`);
``` 
should be
``` js
Module['setStatus'](`${message} (${expected - remaining}/${expected})`);
```

Follow-up to #28090. Records in `GL.offscreenCanvases` no longer have `canvasSharedPtr`, but `__pthread_create_js` still writes the owning thread to it for every forwarded record. On wasm32 that writes the pthread pointer to address 0. This adds a check before the write.

It needs a canvas with an explicitSwapControl context, passed to `pthread_create` as a transferred canvas:

```c
EmscriptenWebGLContextAttributes a;
emscripten_webgl_init_context_attributes(&a);
a.explicitSwapControl = true;
emscripten_webgl_destroy_context(emscripten_webgl_create_context("#canvas", &a));
pthread_attr_t attr;
pthread_attr_init(&attr);
emscripten_pthread_attr_settransferredcanvases(&attr, "#canvas");
pthread_create(&t, &attr, fn, NULL);
```

Built with `-pthread -sOFFSCREENCANVAS_SUPPORT -sPTHREAD_POOL_SIZE=1 -lGL`, on Chrome 151 headless:

| Build | main | This PR |
|---|---|---|
| Assertions on | `Aborted(Runtime error: The application has corrupted its heap memory area (address zero)!)` | `InvalidStateError` |
| `-O2 -sASSERTIONS=0` | `InvalidStateError`, after the write to address 0 | `InvalidStateError` |

The transfer can't succeed either way, since the canvas already has a context. This only keeps the write from corrupting address 0 and hiding the browser's error. I didn't add a test because the expected result is that browser-specific exception. I haven't run this on Firefox or Safari.

_Made with AI assistance under my review._

